WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.429
Film Courage: How does a screenwriter know
when it’s time to sell their screenplay?

00:00:03.429 --> 00:00:08.140
Daniel Calvisi, screenwriter: Well you want
to show it to a bunch of friends.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:14.700
You want to get notes from friends who know
screenwriting, who know movies and know how

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.070
to give notes on a script.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:19.680
So ideally you’re in a writer’s group
and you’ve got feedback from your writer’s

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.680
group.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.930
You’ve written (I would say) at least five
or six drafts.

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.840
Writing it re-writing as we know.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:32.140
And hopefully maybe you’ve shown it to someone
you’ve known in the industry, not as a submission

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:35.650
but as a friend to read, where they’ve given
you some feedback.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:43.770
So there’s really no way to know exactly
when you’re going to be ready to bring something

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:49.620
to market but basically field-test it with
as many people as you can to the point where

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.730
you feel like it’s in its best shape as
possible.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:57.640
Film Courage: Okay, let’s suppose friends,
family say “Oh this is excellent!”

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.590
Now we know that they have their reasons for
saying this, as well.

00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:01.899
They don’t want to hurt our feelings.

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:04.049
They want to see us do well.

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:09.189
Let’s suppose we’re ready to try and go
out there and take it to market, what are

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:13.429
some of the of the first things, what are
some websites that we can go to, what can

00:01:13.429 --> 00:01:15.100
we do to try and help sell it?

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:19.959
Daniel: I think you should make a hit list
of companies and people that you’re going

00:01:19.959 --> 00:01:21.729
to send your script to.

00:01:21.729 --> 00:01:24.170
First of all, it’s good to get a rep.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:28.979
So you want to send it to managers that accept
unsolicited submissions.

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:31.890
I believe The Writer’s Guild has a list
of those.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:36.529
There’s a website called The Tracking Board
where you can probably find some good manager

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:37.679
lists.

00:01:37.679 --> 00:01:41.439
But I always say pick up the phone.

00:01:41.439 --> 00:01:43.069
That’s the big thing now.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:46.419
We don’t make as many phone calls as we
used to.

00:01:46.419 --> 00:01:51.950
It makes us all a little nervous and if you’re
a skittish writer who is more of an introvert

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.200
that’s tough.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:58.459
But you have to pick up the phone and you
can actually get submissions just from cold-calling

00:01:58.459 --> 00:02:03.549
and introducing yourself and being nice and
saying “Hey, here is what my script is about,

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:05.950
here is the log line, do you want to read
it?”

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:11.849
Now they may shut you down right away and
say “We don’t accept any unsolicited submissions”

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:17.819
but they may love it and they can send you
a waiver form that you can sign where you

00:02:17.819 --> 00:02:19.900
can submit without a rep.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:24.209
But that’s only if they’ve heard your
log line, they’ve heard your pitch and they

00:02:24.209 --> 00:02:25.920
really like it and they want to read it.

